  'Statistics',
  'Statistics',
  'This Year 2 foundational Kinesiology course prepares students to use statistics as a practical language for exercise science, motor behavior, rehabilitation, public health, and sport-performance evidence. Students learn to describe data, quantify uncertainty, test claims, model relationships, use statistical software, and interpret research findings responsibly.',
  $statjson${"slug": "statistics", "title": "Statistics", "description": "This Year 2 foundational Kinesiology course prepares students to use statistics as a practical language for exercise science, motor behavior, rehabilitation, public health, and sport-performance evidence. Students learn to describe data, quantify uncertainty, test claims, model relationships, use statistical software, and interpret research findings responsibly.", "topics": [{"id": "7b1046ea-aebe-49d0-b8a2-56d3bfffba26", "type": "topic", "title": "Statistical thinking for kinesiology and health sciences", "description": "Statistics helps kinesiology students move from anecdotes to evidence. This section frames statistics as a decision tool for research, clinical reasoning, coaching, performance, and public health.", "children": [{"id": "f4e74a3e-aaf3-404f-8b38-85d0a8e86b98", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Evidence, uncertainty, and decision-making", "description": "Students learn why data never speaks alone; it must be interpreted with uncertainty, context, and professional judgment.", "children": [{"id": "859c7018-e904-4885-86dd-e44156470e1d", "type": "concept", "title": "Population versus sample reasoning"}, {"id": "1fc1ceae-bf1f-421f-b0b1-3ee13c1ae149", "type": "concept", "title": "Association, prediction, causation, and decision support"}, {"id": "2b01d183-b6a2-4a30-ab36-509979baa34a", "type": "concept", "title": "Statistical versus practical questions"}, {"id": "ab554a4f-ca5c-4fff-bfee-b2ca9e7d4bf3", "type": "concept", "title": "Examples from exercise testing, injury prevention, rehabilitation, and sport science"}]}, {"id": "e2a2b88d-e862-4e1e-9676-46cbfd58e4cf", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Research questions and statistical claims", "description": "The point is to match the statistical method to the question being asked.", "children": [{"id": "1e3ddc29-5580-479a-adfd-0c4354949c2b", "type": "concept", "title": "Describing groups, estimating effects, comparing interventions, and modeling relationships"}, {"id": "c2a0eae8-2f5c-41ab-a029-07b917e1e7a7", "type": "concept", "title": "Outcome, exposure, predictor, response, and covariate language"}, {"id": "85b3d034-8af3-4e3d-8984-cbae1f687d13", "type": "concept", "title": "Operational definitions and measurement choices"}, {"id": "74c15b84-48de-48b1-b206-bd63cce07e8b", "type": "concept", "title": "Common statistical claims in kinesiology literature"}]}]}, {"id": "5bb77e5d-921c-49f5-b732-50990c355a9e", "type": "topic", "title": "Data types, data quality, visualization, and descriptive statistics", "description": "Before inference comes description. This section teaches students to understand what kind of data they have, whether it is trustworthy, and how to summarize it clearly.", "children": [{"id": "38303169-3dd1-4bfb-94a6-71d270fd9397", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Variables, data quality, and visualization", "description": "Students learn to inspect datasets before calculating or testing anything.", "children": [{"id": "d5fbd662-a8ad-489b-a667-e3c60d27b190", "type": "concept", "title": "Categorical, ordinal, interval, ratio, count, and time-to-event variables"}, {"id": "c7829d69-a143-4572-b084-d2d9c2209f7d", "type": "concept", "title": "Missing data, impossible values, outliers, duplicates, and coding errors"}, {"id": "9c96cc9d-d825-4a2e-8b3b-f7993dce5a88", "type": "concept", "title": "Histograms, boxplots, scatterplots, bar charts, and line plots"}, {"id": "5ef7f81d-1a17-4b35-8eca-232c1e9c0c87", "type": "concept", "title": "Misleading graphs and ethical visualization choices"}]}, {"id": "bcb71fa4-177a-4519-99e9-a645eb4e709c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Descriptive summaries", "description": "The gist is to summarize data in ways that match the variable and distribution.", "children": [{"id": "c3e75dda-8f0c-4e7e-bb6a-369aee4ffa93", "type": "concept", "title": "Mean, median, mode, percentiles, and proportions"}, {"id": "971930b7-6dfe-41e3-88e8-ba044098ed66", "type": "concept", "title": "Range, interquartile range, variance, standard deviation, and coefficient of variation"}, {"id": "10a60eb8-1c72-424b-bf97-e8d2d0422205", "type": "concept", "title": "Skewness, distribution shape, floor/ceiling effects, and transformations"}, {"id": "2039587b-cd01-40ae-bc94-c4b29dce1f70", "type": "concept", "title": "Reporting descriptive results for health and performance data"}]}]}, {"id": "03433f75-b907-4ac0-b7af-0ff8987051c5", "type": "topic", "title": "Probability, uncertainty, and diagnostic reasoning", "description": "Probability gives students a language for uncertainty. In kinesiology and health sciences, it supports risk interpretation, screening decisions, and evidence-based judgment.", "children": [{"id": "e86023b6-f615-403b-bd8f-453b453f248d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Core probability concepts", "description": "Students learn the rules behind risk, chance, and conditional statements.", "children": [{"id": "ccf46e67-4f30-461d-8b29-d46ad4e44f18", "type": "concept", "title": "Events, complements, unions, intersections, and independence"}, {"id": "f8369baf-d208-4b13-9603-28ee77289e9a", "type": "concept", "title": "Conditional probability and Bayes-style reasoning"}, {"id": "d5f99538-d0fa-4cb4-bbdc-fdc6ff080b42", "type": "concept", "title": "Expected value and variability"}, {"id": "d1240b3f-977f-4f14-a256-4a194fb04e90", "type": "concept", "title": "Probability statements in sport, health, and rehabilitation contexts"}]}, {"id": "c4aa9055-7bb5-4fd1-b0fd-f5daed578b96", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Screening and diagnostic reasoning", "description": "The point is to understand how test accuracy and prevalence affect interpretation.", "children": [{"id": "1f0d576c-cd56-47cc-8906-99c3895912ad", "type": "concept", "title": "Sensitivity, specificity, false positives, and false negatives"}, {"id": "c372c71f-9945-4c96-adc3-af482f278e7c", "type": "concept", "title": "Positive and negative predictive value"}, {"id": "bfc146b5-d0a7-492d-addf-6b89e2ac2f86", "type": "concept", "title": "Likelihood ratios and pretest probability intuition"}, {"id": "c227616c-1467-48bd-8ac2-09cb16c6238d", "type": "concept", "title": "Screening examples from fitness testing, concussion, injury risk, and health appraisal"}]}]}, {"id": "86057e08-38e8-4c09-bc57-bfb24a792115", "type": "topic", "title": "Distributions, standard scores, and sampling variability", "description": "Distributions connect raw scores to probabilities and inference. This section builds the foundation for confidence intervals, hypothesis tests, and model assumptions.", "children": [{"id": "07c4229c-36af-4684-b5f2-fd4aa3c88b5d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Common distributions and standard scores", "description": "Students learn why different statistical methods rely on different reference distributions.", "children": [{"id": "4355ac28-787c-447c-bd2b-4ca92817d71c", "type": "concept", "title": "Normal, t, chi-square, F, and binomial distributions"}, {"id": "0f26be03-9d7f-45fb-9490-683b3b95e9a9", "type": "concept", "title": "z-scores, percentiles, and standardized comparisons"}, {"id": "338c5d87-4607-4f7c-9561-59e73f7ce6c3", "type": "concept", "title": "Distributional assumptions and when they matter"}, {"id": "c3276b70-1f12-45ec-80f6-965e3a1f4465", "type": "concept", "title": "Examples using VO2, strength, reaction time, and injury counts"}]}, {"id": "1f754bdc-0ddc-4b2f-ad4c-052339951fda", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Sampling variability and standard error", "description": "The gist is to understand why samples differ and how that uncertainty is quantified.", "children": [{"id": "85e4ee7d-24ad-49b6-b0ea-7f51f5788436", "type": "concept", "title": "Sampling distributions and the central limit theorem"}, {"id": "c4c6e40d-fdec-4764-9821-637888ce876c", "type": "concept", "title": "Standard error of a mean, proportion, and regression estimate"}, {"id": "39fff5a4-dd63-49d3-b8a6-1135361d2d82", "type": "concept", "title": "Sample size and variability effects"}, {"id": "9b970bac-64a8-45d4-9535-6eb603b0c205", "type": "concept", "title": "Simulation as a way to see sampling behavior"}]}]}, {"id": "78b81ffb-c19b-4b3a-ac4b-2e05bb447098", "type": "topic", "title": "Sampling, study design, bias, and measurement error", "description": "Good statistics cannot rescue weak design. This section helps students see how sampling, design, and measurement quality shape what can be concluded.", "children": [{"id": "2ed91f6f-910e-45be-b3c3-fee36c233d4f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Sampling and study design", "description": "Students learn how design choices determine the strength and limits of evidence.", "children": [{"id": "92b8f557-6771-4495-9efb-7b82c69df5d1", "type": "concept", "title": "Random, convenience, stratified, cluster, and purposive sampling"}, {"id": "a2d4591a-b3e9-47d5-8046-4c3e3b8afaf4", "type": "concept", "title": "Experimental, quasi-experimental, cohort, case-control, and cross-sectional designs"}, {"id": "51674f78-6e73-4411-acb4-1caee20e163d", "type": "concept", "title": "Confounding, randomization, blinding, and control groups"}, {"id": "8f1302fc-0aea-4c8f-bd2b-8d56b096ce95", "type": "concept", "title": "External validity and representation in kinesiology research"}]}, {"id": "b35870bd-5815-41b6-a445-34468296bcb6", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Bias, validity, reliability, and error", "description": "The point is to distinguish true effects from artifacts of measurement or design.", "children": [{"id": "c0cd0864-589c-4d8d-857b-4a5bead9305c", "type": "concept", "title": "Selection bias, information bias, recall bias, and attrition"}, {"id": "4484bdf9-8b23-46c7-9dc6-5207e5a5708d", "type": "concept", "title": "Reliability, validity, standard error of measurement, and minimal detectable change"}, {"id": "28b48c85-3194-4d99-96bd-92a6ee421d3b", "type": "concept", "title": "Inter-rater and intra-rater reliability"}, {"id": "1fba28d2-d436-432a-a0f8-1b35c3b5af8e", "type": "concept", "title": "Instrument calibration and protocol standardization"}]}]}, {"id": "b6da50ee-e128-47c0-ae0b-fc09dfa200e9", "type": "topic", "title": "Confidence intervals, estimation, effect sizes, and practical significance", "description": "Estimation often tells a better story than p-values alone. This section teaches students to communicate magnitude, precision, and real-world importance.", "children": [{"id": "0d5e4908-a87a-4cb3-8c07-5089440232b1", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Confidence intervals and estimation", "description": "Students learn to report uncertainty around estimates instead of treating sample results as exact.", "children": [{"id": "8dd2f0b8-bf3d-4fd7-9094-5c83717d7e92", "type": "concept", "title": "Point estimates and interval estimates"}, {"id": "84d24b5a-b459-46f0-a13d-2cfd5f1342ab", "type": "concept", "title": "Confidence intervals for means, mean differences, proportions, and correlations"}, {"id": "02cfd4d3-d517-41ae-8de7-0c3f7d740e9d", "type": "concept", "title": "Margin of error and sample size effects"}, {"id": "7a2c211d-936c-424b-b8d1-9edcb2efbee1", "type": "concept", "title": "Interpreting intervals without common misconceptions"}]}, {"id": "2591beaa-4667-4c26-9fd2-3fac76a2ccbd", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Effect sizes and meaningful change", "description": "The gist is to ask not only whether an effect exists, but whether it matters.", "children": [{"id": "f6b43845-8fee-4935-86b5-a4dd91e95dee", "type": "concept", "title": "Cohen's d, Hedges' g, eta-squared, r, odds ratios, and risk ratios"}, {"id": "e6a3fa15-746c-484d-841f-1bfaf00f1261", "type": "concept", "title": "Small, medium, large, and context-specific interpretation"}, {"id": "b6c94727-25cf-4bbb-b43d-75e567662cef", "type": "concept", "title": "Minimal clinically important difference and smallest worthwhile change"}, {"id": "ec24f295-4a76-46d2-8006-5d99ee59a674", "type": "concept", "title": "Practical significance in training, rehab, and health promotion"}]}]}, {"id": "33e6fbde-218a-4b6e-9a93-2d7f5904d297", "type": "topic", "title": "Hypothesis testing, p-values, power, and multiple comparisons", "description": "Hypothesis testing is useful but often misunderstood. This section builds correct interpretation and good habits around p-values, errors, power, and overtesting.", "children": [{"id": "a2b8de20-2bf6-41e8-a27b-fd239bddd8bc", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Logic of hypothesis testing", "description": "Students learn what a hypothesis test can and cannot say.", "children": [{"id": "01bd5518-d16f-4d3b-be98-e922fdb753f7", "type": "concept", "title": "Null and alternative hypotheses"}, {"id": "7c2d3740-44a5-4c63-b86c-87a9a2a91a3e", "type": "concept", "title": "Test statistics, p-values, alpha, and decision rules"}, {"id": "b3f10470-4711-4f2e-a7d1-f4af5b404f8a", "type": "concept", "title": "Type I and Type II errors"}, {"id": "fb8659f5-198c-4d5b-a0c5-361a3f028f31", "type": "concept", "title": "One-tailed versus two-tailed tests and preregistered expectations"}]}, {"id": "fb087b78-fb57-42be-b72c-491e2f9e499e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Power, sample size, and multiplicity", "description": "The point is to understand why studies miss real effects or overstate chance findings.", "children": [{"id": "b5837c30-77b0-4c31-a4fd-26889711a49e", "type": "concept", "title": "Statistical power and factors that influence it"}, {"id": "08d73179-c743-47e0-8352-3ee1b00dd262", "type": "concept", "title": "Effect size, variability, alpha, and sample size logic"}, {"id": "fd34730c-e5ed-4d81-8b99-6c1cbc0fac03", "type": "concept", "title": "Multiple comparisons and false discovery risk"}, {"id": "b1b4da42-c555-4218-899b-7c2ebc1a860b", "type": "concept", "title": "Replication, transparency, and avoiding p-hacking"}]}]}, {"id": "d18a06da-cf73-4b70-9aed-4647f4e9731d", "type": "topic", "title": "t-tests, nonparametric alternatives, and group comparisons", "description": "Many kinesiology questions compare two groups or two time points. This section teaches common group-comparison tools and the assumptions behind them.", "children": [{"id": "a9f88def-9469-4daf-9287-4c4e63f06e84", "type": "subtopic", "title": "t-tests and assumptions", "description": "Students learn to choose and interpret one-sample, paired, and independent-samples t-tests.", "children": [{"id": "6d98c02e-4169-4fab-aa5f-d69420e3b2f2", "type": "concept", "title": "One-sample, paired, and independent t-tests"}, {"id": "57c7ff6a-6a62-48d7-8143-3eb4b5289ade", "type": "concept", "title": "Normality, independence, equal variance, and outlier assumptions"}, {"id": "11d24fb0-0635-4c51-b805-a5c75ef18772", "type": "concept", "title": "Mean difference, confidence interval, p-value, and effect size"}, {"id": "68fe8305-aa0f-4ae4-b5f2-cde99c7cbe38", "type": "concept", "title": "Examples using pre/post training, group performance, and health outcomes"}]}, {"id": "3277b061-78cf-4133-930e-2773db3ebd62", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Nonparametric and robust alternatives", "description": "The gist is to know what to do when data do not fit simple parametric assumptions.", "children": [{"id": "39ab8298-f4c9-4e0e-9bfd-03c00503b5db", "type": "concept", "title": "Mann-Whitney U and Wilcoxon signed-rank tests"}, {"id": "af505f49-561e-4253-88dc-0da4c7edf0e5", "type": "concept", "title": "Median, rank, and distribution-shift interpretation"}, {"id": "f34501a1-00c8-44a9-8ed4-68bb6106f90b", "type": "concept", "title": "Bootstrap confidence intervals and permutation-test intuition"}, {"id": "2a6a6ef5-be3c-4662-90a5-00c826528d78", "type": "concept", "title": "Reporting limitations and practical meaning"}]}]}, {"id": "a3063e1c-2b83-449f-a973-0a67040bdd75", "type": "topic", "title": "ANOVA, factorial designs, repeated measures, and post hoc tests", "description": "ANOVA extends group comparison to more complex designs. This section prepares students for intervention, training, and repeated-measure studies common in kinesiology.", "children": [{"id": "e473bdb5-e269-4cd1-8238-babbb34a5557", "type": "subtopic", "title": "ANOVA models and design logic", "description": "Students learn how ANOVA partitions variability and tests group differences.", "children": [{"id": "dcb5a1dd-5959-4d9d-911a-26c06fb2a7ac", "type": "concept", "title": "One-way ANOVA and omnibus tests"}, {"id": "f0afe317-be08-4102-89d9-62fc1c230fe5", "type": "concept", "title": "Factorial designs, main effects, and interactions"}, {"id": "05f0bec0-aa20-4018-b1a8-8bc58cff5062", "type": "concept", "title": "Repeated-measures and mixed-design concepts"}, {"id": "084d19ca-ee8b-4b92-8b5a-254c70890f58", "type": "concept", "title": "Assumptions: independence, normality, homogeneity, and sphericity"}]}, {"id": "e7792e3f-4ccd-48ae-9dd6-c1a05a316f68", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Post hoc tests and interpretation", "description": "The point is to move from omnibus significance to clear, responsible conclusions.", "children": [{"id": "72fffcc0-e21d-4771-99b9-fb661234ffe5", "type": "concept", "title": "Planned contrasts and post hoc comparisons"}, {"id": "05d43683-c78c-45df-9be7-76aea0b511e9", "type": "concept", "title": "Tukey, Bonferroni, and multiplicity control concepts"}, {"id": "d834c378-dd1f-425e-838d-90b480088884", "type": "concept", "title": "Eta-squared, partial eta-squared, and confidence intervals"}, {"id": "3aede4a8-7fa9-43ff-a33d-47129d59b339", "type": "concept", "title": "Interaction plots and communication of findings"}]}]}, {"id": "f6f5d8da-cf43-46fb-a365-3d9f9fa4e01c", "type": "topic", "title": "Correlation, regression, prediction, and model interpretation", "description": "Regression connects variables and supports prediction, but it does not automatically prove causation. This section builds modeling literacy for kinesiology datasets.", "children": [{"id": "fba7e63c-425e-4e85-8d11-dda527f9135f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Correlation and simple regression", "description": "Students learn to quantify association and fit simple predictive models.", "children": [{"id": "84a8927c-05a7-40c3-bb45-cc292a5c6f57", "type": "concept", "title": "Pearson and Spearman correlation"}, {"id": "51b80a92-d648-44c1-8c86-d0e5de7143d1", "type": "concept", "title": "Scatterplots, linearity, outliers, and restriction of range"}, {"id": "22ac9f3c-6225-4b6a-bde4-6b0a069bc4d9", "type": "concept", "title": "Slope, intercept, R-squared, residuals, and prediction error"}, {"id": "f4888f02-7a32-4d6c-ab1c-608c00428836", "type": "concept", "title": "Examples using training load, fitness, strength, gait, and performance"}]}, {"id": "acd5475b-95ec-4abe-b069-9b94b9173445", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Multiple regression and model judgment", "description": "The gist is to interpret models with covariates while watching for overclaiming.", "children": [{"id": "c931bc28-a487-439c-acc3-1939f0976776", "type": "concept", "title": "Confounding, adjustment, and covariate selection"}, {"id": "7d3406c1-0c82-468b-9e1a-fdeecb3c4925", "type": "concept", "title": "Dummy variables and categorical predictors"}, {"id": "9f2f1ae4-58a5-46e2-88dd-35acb5b6ce83", "type": "concept", "title": "Multicollinearity, residual diagnostics, and influential cases"}, {"id": "e7646088-4530-4ddf-968e-ed3e9ba4be36", "type": "concept", "title": "Prediction versus explanation and causality cautions"}]}]}, {"id": "8ee8d941-3afd-4643-a449-cbbfa48d143f", "type": "topic", "title": "Categorical data, epidemiologic measures, and health research interpretation", "description": "Health and performance research often uses categories: injured/not injured, active/inactive, exposed/unexposed. This section adds tools for categorical outcomes and population health reasoning.", "children": [{"id": "127f7f77-f45d-48ae-9661-b583948963f9", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Categorical data analysis", "description": "Students learn to analyze counts, proportions, and group membership.", "children": [{"id": "04223be1-2ac1-4133-a339-6e5ac2931ce2", "type": "concept", "title": "Frequency tables, cross-tabulations, and stacked bar charts"}, {"id": "35df83b6-6758-405d-9ea9-acb6d5e9d4ab", "type": "concept", "title": "Chi-square tests, Fisher's exact test concepts, and expected counts"}, {"id": "6505f264-1a4d-4eea-ab54-fb32071755cc", "type": "concept", "title": "Risk difference, relative risk, odds ratio, prevalence, and incidence"}, {"id": "612c8bd2-4dd7-420a-97b3-4dcf02d353d2", "type": "concept", "title": "Interpreting categorical outcomes in health and sport settings"}]}, {"id": "3ef1dcce-09ab-44ba-91c4-43a9ee85b886", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Evidence appraisal and research interpretation", "description": "The point is to read statistical claims in articles with healthy skepticism.", "children": [{"id": "57732a19-6272-412f-b02e-6455452bf4f1", "type": "concept", "title": "Clinical versus statistical significance"}, {"id": "2348bd8b-58dd-4280-9948-c5fd786079c5", "type": "concept", "title": "Confidence intervals, effect sizes, and external validity in articles"}, {"id": "727abb6d-7af7-4f6c-b874-c2386fa6391a", "type": "concept", "title": "Common misinterpretations in abstracts and media reports"}, {"id": "45c6125e-a8c7-4cc2-b60b-2110f8ef5781", "type": "concept", "title": "CONSORT/STROBE-style reporting awareness"}]}]}, {"id": "40fabb41-fefc-40d1-b356-d97f3bea955e", "type": "topic", "title": "Statistical software, reproducible workflows, reporting, and research communication", "description": "Modern statistics is also a workflow. This section helps students use software responsibly, organize data, and communicate results in research-ready formats.", "children": [{"id": "4df16149-55b1-4b2a-93c5-ab3ef2cb2ccf", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Software workflows and reproducibility", "description": "Students learn practical habits that make analyses checkable and reusable.", "children": [{"id": "941baf51-70d9-4ca6-8839-2399c04b9c5a", "type": "concept", "title": "Spreadsheet hygiene, codebooks, data dictionaries, and versioning"}, {"id": "d5ab12ba-87cd-4efe-a36b-4865cf05ebec", "type": "concept", "title": "R, SPSS, JASP, jamovi, or spreadsheet analysis concepts"}, {"id": "ef0bd135-a211-452e-aba7-be4bf2cf27a9", "type": "concept", "title": "Data cleaning, recoding, formulas, scripts, and output verification"}, {"id": "4c5ec39e-4a10-40d6-b590-a1881ba6d416", "type": "concept", "title": "Reproducibility, file organization, and transparent decision logs"}]}, {"id": "94815173-6ad3-46cb-8de7-0b7f525e6f01", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Reporting and communication", "description": "The gist is to turn analysis into clear tables, figures, and written conclusions.", "children": [{"id": "e9e49827-123b-4128-8044-4d6da407def9", "type": "concept", "title": "APA and health-science reporting conventions"}, {"id": "ae51aeb5-a8ce-4122-bf7e-dc554f74cd3b", "type": "concept", "title": "Tables, figures, captions, and statistical notation"}, {"id": "88da0012-6439-401a-a583-93d5e1e4af24", "type": "concept", "title": "Plain-language conclusions with limitations, assumptions, and uncertainty"}]}]}], "dbBacked": true}$statjson$::jsonb,
  true,
  false,
  'course',
  'public'
where not exists (
  select 1 from public.learning_paths where slug = 'statistics'
);

update public.learning_paths
set
  title = 'Statistics',
  goal = 'Statistics',
  summary = 'This Year 2 foundational Kinesiology course prepares students to use statistics as a practical language for exercise science, motor behavior, rehabilitation, public health, and sport-performance evidence. Students learn to describe data, quantify uncertainty, test claims, model relationships, use statistical software, and interpret research findings responsibly.',
  data = $statjson${"slug": "statistics", "title": "Statistics", "description": "This Year 2 foundational Kinesiology course prepares students to use statistics as a practical language for exercise science, motor behavior, rehabilitation, public health, and sport-performance evidence. Students learn to describe data, quantify uncertainty, test claims, model relationships, use statistical software, and interpret research findings responsibly.", "topics": [{"id": "7b1046ea-aebe-49d0-b8a2-56d3bfffba26", "type": "topic", "title": "Statistical thinking for kinesiology and health sciences", "description": "Statistics helps kinesiology students move from anecdotes to evidence. This section frames statistics as a decision tool for research, clinical reasoning, coaching, performance, and public health.", "children": [{"id": "f4e74a3e-aaf3-404f-8b38-85d0a8e86b98", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Evidence, uncertainty, and decision-making", "description": "Students learn why data never speaks alone; it must be interpreted with uncertainty, context, and professional judgment.", "children": [{"id": "859c7018-e904-4885-86dd-e44156470e1d", "type": "concept", "title": "Population versus sample reasoning"}, {"id": "1fc1ceae-bf1f-421f-b0b1-3ee13c1ae149", "type": "concept", "title": "Association, prediction, causation, and decision support"}, {"id": "2b01d183-b6a2-4a30-ab36-509979baa34a", "type": "concept", "title": "Statistical versus practical questions"}, {"id": "ab554a4f-ca5c-4fff-bfee-b2ca9e7d4bf3", "type": "concept", "title": "Examples from exercise testing, injury prevention, rehabilitation, and sport science"}]}, {"id": "e2a2b88d-e862-4e1e-9676-46cbfd58e4cf", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Research questions and statistical claims", "description": "The point is to match the statistical method to the question being asked.", "children": [{"id": "1e3ddc29-5580-479a-adfd-0c4354949c2b", "type": "concept", "title": "Describing groups, estimating effects, comparing interventions, and modeling relationships"}, {"id": "c2a0eae8-2f5c-41ab-a029-07b917e1e7a7", "type": "concept", "title": "Outcome, exposure, predictor, response, and covariate language"}, {"id": "85b3d034-8af3-4e3d-8984-cbae1f687d13", "type": "concept", "title": "Operational definitions and measurement choices"}, {"id": "74c15b84-48de-48b1-b206-bd63cce07e8b", "type": "concept", "title": "Common statistical claims in kinesiology literature"}]}]}, {"id": "5bb77e5d-921c-49f5-b732-50990c355a9e", "type": "topic", "title": "Data types, data quality, visualization, and descriptive statistics", "description": "Before inference comes description. This section teaches students to understand what kind of data they have, whether it is trustworthy, and how to summarize it clearly.", "children": [{"id": "38303169-3dd1-4bfb-94a6-71d270fd9397", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Variables, data quality, and visualization", "description": "Students learn to inspect datasets before calculating or testing anything.", "children": [{"id": "d5fbd662-a8ad-489b-a667-e3c60d27b190", "type": "concept", "title": "Categorical, ordinal, interval, ratio, count, and time-to-event variables"}, {"id": "c7829d69-a143-4572-b084-d2d9c2209f7d", "type": "concept", "title": "Missing data, impossible values, outliers, duplicates, and coding errors"}, {"id": "9c96cc9d-d825-4a2e-8b3b-f7993dce5a88", "type": "concept", "title": "Histograms, boxplots, scatterplots, bar charts, and line plots"}, {"id": "5ef7f81d-1a17-4b35-8eca-232c1e9c0c87", "type": "concept", "title": "Misleading graphs and ethical visualization choices"}]}, {"id": "bcb71fa4-177a-4519-99e9-a645eb4e709c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Descriptive summaries", "description": "The gist is to summarize data in ways that match the variable and distribution.", "children": [{"id": "c3e75dda-8f0c-4e7e-bb6a-369aee4ffa93", "type": "concept", "title": "Mean, median, mode, percentiles, and proportions"}, {"id": "971930b7-6dfe-41e3-88e8-ba044098ed66", "type": "concept", "title": "Range, interquartile range, variance, standard deviation, and coefficient of variation"}, {"id": "10a60eb8-1c72-424b-bf97-e8d2d0422205", "type": "concept", "title": "Skewness, distribution shape, floor/ceiling effects, and transformations"}, {"id": "2039587b-cd01-40ae-bc94-c4b29dce1f70", "type": "concept", "title": "Reporting descriptive results for health and performance data"}]}]}, {"id": "03433f75-b907-4ac0-b7af-0ff8987051c5", "type": "topic", "title": "Probability, uncertainty, and diagnostic reasoning", "description": "Probability gives students a language for uncertainty. In kinesiology and health sciences, it supports risk interpretation, screening decisions, and evidence-based judgment.", "children": [{"id": "e86023b6-f615-403b-bd8f-453b453f248d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Core probability concepts", "description": "Students learn the rules behind risk, chance, and conditional statements.", "children": [{"id": "ccf46e67-4f30-461d-8b29-d46ad4e44f18", "type": "concept", "title": "Events, complements, unions, intersections, and independence"}, {"id": "f8369baf-d208-4b13-9603-28ee77289e9a", "type": "concept", "title": "Conditional probability and Bayes-style reasoning"}, {"id": "d5f99538-d0fa-4cb4-bbdc-fdc6ff080b42", "type": "concept", "title": "Expected value and variability"}, {"id": "d1240b3f-977f-4f14-a256-4a194fb04e90", "type": "concept", "title": "Probability statements in sport, health, and rehabilitation contexts"}]}, {"id": "c4aa9055-7bb5-4fd1-b0fd-f5daed578b96", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Screening and diagnostic reasoning", "description": "The point is to understand how test accuracy and prevalence affect interpretation.", "children": [{"id": "1f0d576c-cd56-47cc-8906-99c3895912ad", "type": "concept", "title": "Sensitivity, specificity, false positives, and false negatives"}, {"id": "c372c71f-9945-4c96-adc3-af482f278e7c", "type": "concept", "title": "Positive and negative predictive value"}, {"id": "bfc146b5-d0a7-492d-addf-6b89e2ac2f86", "type": "concept", "title": "Likelihood ratios and pretest probability intuition"}, {"id": "c227616c-1467-48bd-8ac2-09cb16c6238d", "type": "concept", "title": "Screening examples from fitness testing, concussion, injury risk, and health appraisal"}]}]}, {"id": "86057e08-38e8-4c09-bc57-bfb24a792115", "type": "topic", "title": "Distributions, standard scores, and sampling variability", "description": "Distributions connect raw scores to probabilities and inference. This section builds the foundation for confidence intervals, hypothesis tests, and model assumptions.", "children": [{"id": "07c4229c-36af-4684-b5f2-fd4aa3c88b5d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Common distributions and standard scores", "description": "Students learn why different statistical methods rely on different reference distributions.", "children": [{"id": "4355ac28-787c-447c-bd2b-4ca92817d71c", "type": "concept", "title": "Normal, t, chi-square, F, and binomial distributions"}, {"id": "0f26be03-9d7f-45fb-9490-683b3b95e9a9", "type": "concept", "title": "z-scores, percentiles, and standardized comparisons"}, {"id": "338c5d87-4607-4f7c-9561-59e73f7ce6c3", "type": "concept", "title": "Distributional assumptions and when they matter"}, {"id": "c3276b70-1f12-45ec-80f6-965e3a1f4465", "type": "concept", "title": "Examples using VO2, strength, reaction time, and injury counts"}]}, {"id": "1f754bdc-0ddc-4b2f-ad4c-052339951fda", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Sampling variability and standard error", "description": "The gist is to understand why samples differ and how that uncertainty is quantified.", "children": [{"id": "85e4ee7d-24ad-49b6-b0ea-7f51f5788436", "type": "concept", "title": "Sampling distributions and the central limit theorem"}, {"id": "c4c6e40d-fdec-4764-9821-637888ce876c", "type": "concept", "title": "Standard error of a mean, proportion, and regression estimate"}, {"id": "39fff5a4-dd63-49d3-b8a6-1135361d2d82", "type": "concept", "title": "Sample size and variability effects"}, {"id": "9b970bac-64a8-45d4-9535-6eb603b0c205", "type": "concept", "title": "Simulation as a way to see sampling behavior"}]}]}, {"id": "78b81ffb-c19b-4b3a-ac4b-2e05bb447098", "type": "topic", "title": "Sampling, study design, bias, and measurement error", "description": "Good statistics cannot rescue weak design. This section helps students see how sampling, design, and measurement quality shape what can be concluded.", "children": [{"id": "2ed91f6f-910e-45be-b3c3-fee36c233d4f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Sampling and study design", "description": "Students learn how design choices determine the strength and limits of evidence.", "children": [{"id": "92b8f557-6771-4495-9efb-7b82c69df5d1", "type": "concept", "title": "Random, convenience, stratified, cluster, and purposive sampling"}, {"id": "a2d4591a-b3e9-47d5-8046-4c3e3b8afaf4", "type": "concept", "title": "Experimental, quasi-experimental, cohort, case-control, and cross-sectional designs"}, {"id": "51674f78-6e73-4411-acb4-1caee20e163d", "type": "concept", "title": "Confounding, randomization, blinding, and control groups"}, {"id": "8f1302fc-0aea-4c8f-bd2b-8d56b096ce95", "type": "concept", "title": "External validity and representation in kinesiology research"}]}, {"id": "b35870bd-5815-41b6-a445-34468296bcb6", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Bias, validity, reliability, and error", "description": "The point is to distinguish true effects from artifacts of measurement or design.", "children": [{"id": "c0cd0864-589c-4d8d-857b-4a5bead9305c", "type": "concept", "title": "Selection bias, information bias, recall bias, and attrition"}, {"id": "4484bdf9-8b23-46c7-9dc6-5207e5a5708d", "type": "concept", "title": "Reliability, validity, standard error of measurement, and minimal detectable change"}, {"id": "28b48c85-3194-4d99-96bd-92a6ee421d3b", "type": "concept", "title": "Inter-rater and intra-rater reliability"}, {"id": "1fba28d2-d436-432a-a0f8-1b35c3b5af8e", "type": "concept", "title": "Instrument calibration and protocol standardization"}]}]}, {"id": "b6da50ee-e128-47c0-ae0b-fc09dfa200e9", "type": "topic", "title": "Confidence intervals, estimation, effect sizes, and practical significance", "description": "Estimation often tells a better story than p-values alone. This section teaches students to communicate magnitude, precision, and real-world importance.", "children": [{"id": "0d5e4908-a87a-4cb3-8c07-5089440232b1", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Confidence intervals and estimation", "description": "Students learn to report uncertainty around estimates instead of treating sample results as exact.", "children": [{"id": "8dd2f0b8-bf3d-4fd7-9094-5c83717d7e92", "type": "concept", "title": "Point estimates and interval estimates"}, {"id": "84d24b5a-b459-46f0-a13d-2cfd5f1342ab", "type": "concept", "title": "Confidence intervals for means, mean differences, proportions, and correlations"}, {"id": "02cfd4d3-d517-41ae-8de7-0c3f7d740e9d", "type": "concept", "title": "Margin of error and sample size effects"}, {"id": "7a2c211d-936c-424b-b8d1-9edcb2efbee1", "type": "concept", "title": "Interpreting intervals without common misconceptions"}]}, {"id": "2591beaa-4667-4c26-9fd2-3fac76a2ccbd", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Effect sizes and meaningful change", "description": "The gist is to ask not only whether an effect exists, but whether it matters.", "children": [{"id": "f6b43845-8fee-4935-86b5-a4dd91e95dee", "type": "concept", "title": "Cohen's d, Hedges' g, eta-squared, r, odds ratios, and risk ratios"}, {"id": "e6a3fa15-746c-484d-841f-1bfaf00f1261", "type": "concept", "title": "Small, medium, large, and context-specific interpretation"}, {"id": "b6c94727-25cf-4bbb-b43d-75e567662cef", "type": "concept", "title": "Minimal clinically important difference and smallest worthwhile change"}, {"id": "ec24f295-4a76-46d2-8006-5d99ee59a674", "type": "concept", "title": "Practical significance in training, rehab, and health promotion"}]}]}, {"id": "33e6fbde-218a-4b6e-9a93-2d7f5904d297", "type": "topic", "title": "Hypothesis testing, p-values, power, and multiple comparisons", "description": "Hypothesis testing is useful but often misunderstood. This section builds correct interpretation and good habits around p-values, errors, power, and overtesting.", "children": [{"id": "a2b8de20-2bf6-41e8-a27b-fd239bddd8bc", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Logic of hypothesis testing", "description": "Students learn what a hypothesis test can and cannot say.", "children": [{"id": "01bd5518-d16f-4d3b-be98-e922fdb753f7", "type": "concept", "title": "Null and alternative hypotheses"}, {"id": "7c2d3740-44a5-4c63-b86c-87a9a2a91a3e", "type": "concept", "title": "Test statistics, p-values, alpha, and decision rules"}, {"id": "b3f10470-4711-4f2e-a7d1-f4af5b404f8a", "type": "concept", "title": "Type I and Type II errors"}, {"id": "fb8659f5-198c-4d5b-a0c5-361a3f028f31", "type": "concept", "title": "One-tailed versus two-tailed tests and preregistered expectations"}]}, {"id": "fb087b78-fb57-42be-b72c-491e2f9e499e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Power, sample size, and multiplicity", "description": "The point is to understand why studies miss real effects or overstate chance findings.", "children": [{"id": "b5837c30-77b0-4c31-a4fd-26889711a49e", "type": "concept", "title": "Statistical power and factors that influence it"}, {"id": "08d73179-c743-47e0-8352-3ee1b00dd262", "type": "concept", "title": "Effect size, variability, alpha, and sample size logic"}, {"id": "fd34730c-e5ed-4d81-8b99-6c1cbc0fac03", "type": "concept", "title": "Multiple comparisons and false discovery risk"}, {"id": "b1b4da42-c555-4218-899b-7c2ebc1a860b", "type": "concept", "title": "Replication, transparency, and avoiding p-hacking"}]}]}, {"id": "d18a06da-cf73-4b70-9aed-4647f4e9731d", "type": "topic", "title": "t-tests, nonparametric alternatives, and group comparisons", "description": "Many kinesiology questions compare two groups or two time points. This section teaches common group-comparison tools and the assumptions behind them.", "children": [{"id": "a9f88def-9469-4daf-9287-4c4e63f06e84", "type": "subtopic", "title": "t-tests and assumptions", "description": "Students learn to choose and interpret one-sample, paired, and independent-samples t-tests.", "children": [{"id": "6d98c02e-4169-4fab-aa5f-d69420e3b2f2", "type": "concept", "title": "One-sample, paired, and independent t-tests"}, {"id": "57c7ff6a-6a62-48d7-8143-3eb4b5289ade", "type": "concept", "title": "Normality, independence, equal variance, and outlier assumptions"}, {"id": "11d24fb0-0635-4c51-b805-a5c75ef18772", "type": "concept", "title": "Mean difference, confidence interval, p-value, and effect size"}, {"id": "68fe8305-aa0f-4ae4-b5f2-cde99c7cbe38", "type": "concept", "title": "Examples using pre/post training, group performance, and health outcomes"}]}, {"id": "3277b061-78cf-4133-930e-2773db3ebd62", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Nonparametric and robust alternatives", "description": "The gist is to know what to do when data do not fit simple parametric assumptions.", "children": [{"id": "39ab8298-f4c9-4e0e-9bfd-03c00503b5db", "type": "concept", "title": "Mann-Whitney U and Wilcoxon signed-rank tests"}, {"id": "af505f49-561e-4253-88dc-0da4c7edf0e5", "type": "concept", "title": "Median, rank, and distribution-shift interpretation"}, {"id": "f34501a1-00c8-44a9-8ed4-68bb6106f90b", "type": "concept", "title": "Bootstrap confidence intervals and permutation-test intuition"}, {"id": "2a6a6ef5-be3c-4662-90a5-00c826528d78", "type": "concept", "title": "Reporting limitations and practical meaning"}]}]}, {"id": "a3063e1c-2b83-449f-a973-0a67040bdd75", "type": "topic", "title": "ANOVA, factorial designs, repeated measures, and post hoc tests", "description": "ANOVA extends group comparison to more complex designs. This section prepares students for intervention, training, and repeated-measure studies common in kinesiology.", "children": [{"id": "e473bdb5-e269-4cd1-8238-babbb34a5557", "type": "subtopic", "title": "ANOVA models and design logic", "description": "Students learn how ANOVA partitions variability and tests group differences.", "children": [{"id": "dcb5a1dd-5959-4d9d-911a-26c06fb2a7ac", "type": "concept", "title": "One-way ANOVA and omnibus tests"}, {"id": "f0afe317-be08-4102-89d9-62fc1c230fe5", "type": "concept", "title": "Factorial designs, main effects, and interactions"}, {"id": "05f0bec0-aa20-4018-b1a8-8bc58cff5062", "type": "concept", "title": "Repeated-measures and mixed-design concepts"}, {"id": "084d19ca-ee8b-4b92-8b5a-254c70890f58", "type": "concept", "title": "Assumptions: independence, normality, homogeneity, and sphericity"}]}, {"id": "e7792e3f-4ccd-48ae-9dd6-c1a05a316f68", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Post hoc tests and interpretation", "description": "The point is to move from omnibus significance to clear, responsible conclusions.", "children": [{"id": "72fffcc0-e21d-4771-99b9-fb661234ffe5", "type": "concept", "title": "Planned contrasts and post hoc comparisons"}, {"id": "05d43683-c78c-45df-9be7-76aea0b511e9", "type": "concept", "title": "Tukey, Bonferroni, and multiplicity control concepts"}, {"id": "d834c378-dd1f-425e-838d-90b480088884", "type": "concept", "title": "Eta-squared, partial eta-squared, and confidence intervals"}, {"id": "3aede4a8-7fa9-43ff-a33d-47129d59b339", "type": "concept", "title": "Interaction plots and communication of findings"}]}]}, {"id": "f6f5d8da-cf43-46fb-a365-3d9f9fa4e01c", "type": "topic", "title": "Correlation, regression, prediction, and model interpretation", "description": "Regression connects variables and supports prediction, but it does not automatically prove causation. This section builds modeling literacy for kinesiology datasets.", "children": [{"id": "fba7e63c-425e-4e85-8d11-dda527f9135f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Correlation and simple regression", "description": "Students learn to quantify association and fit simple predictive models.", "children": [{"id": "84a8927c-05a7-40c3-bb45-cc292a5c6f57", "type": "concept", "title": "Pearson and Spearman correlation"}, {"id": "51b80a92-d648-44c1-8c86-d0e5de7143d1", "type": "concept", "title": "Scatterplots, linearity, outliers, and restriction of range"}, {"id": "22ac9f3c-6225-4b6a-bde4-6b0a069bc4d9", "type": "concept", "title": "Slope, intercept, R-squared, residuals, and prediction error"}, {"id": "f4888f02-7a32-4d6c-ab1c-608c00428836", "type": "concept", "title": "Examples using training load, fitness, strength, gait, and performance"}]}, {"id": "acd5475b-95ec-4abe-b069-9b94b9173445", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Multiple regression and model judgment", "description": "The gist is to interpret models with covariates while watching for overclaiming.", "children": [{"id": "c931bc28-a487-439c-acc3-1939f0976776", "type": "concept", "title": "Confounding, adjustment, and covariate selection"}, {"id": "7d3406c1-0c82-468b-9e1a-fdeecb3c4925", "type": "concept", "title": "Dummy variables and categorical predictors"}, {"id": "9f2f1ae4-58a5-46e2-88dd-35acb5b6ce83", "type": "concept", "title": "Multicollinearity, residual diagnostics, and influential cases"}, {"id": "e7646088-4530-4ddf-968e-ed3e9ba4be36", "type": "concept", "title": "Prediction versus explanation and causality cautions"}]}]}, {"id": "8ee8d941-3afd-4643-a449-cbbfa48d143f", "type": "topic", "title": "Categorical data, epidemiologic measures, and health research interpretation", "description": "Health and performance research often uses categories: injured/not injured, active/inactive, exposed/unexposed. This section adds tools for categorical outcomes and population health reasoning.", "children": [{"id": "127f7f77-f45d-48ae-9661-b583948963f9", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Categorical data analysis", "description": "Students learn to analyze counts, proportions, and group membership.", "children": [{"id": "04223be1-2ac1-4133-a339-6e5ac2931ce2", "type": "concept", "title": "Frequency tables, cross-tabulations, and stacked bar charts"}, {"id": "35df83b6-6758-405d-9ea9-acb6d5e9d4ab", "type": "concept", "title": "Chi-square tests, Fisher's exact test concepts, and expected counts"}, {"id": "6505f264-1a4d-4eea-ab54-fb32071755cc", "type": "concept", "title": "Risk difference, relative risk, odds ratio, prevalence, and incidence"}, {"id": "612c8bd2-4dd7-420a-97b3-4dcf02d353d2", "type": "concept", "title": "Interpreting categorical outcomes in health and sport settings"}]}, {"id": "3ef1dcce-09ab-44ba-91c4-43a9ee85b886", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Evidence appraisal and research interpretation", "description": "The point is to read statistical claims in articles with healthy skepticism.", "children": [{"id": "57732a19-6272-412f-b02e-6455452bf4f1", "type": "concept", "title": "Clinical versus statistical significance"}, {"id": "2348bd8b-58dd-4280-9948-c5fd786079c5", "type": "concept", "title": "Confidence intervals, effect sizes, and external validity in articles"}, {"id": "727abb6d-7af7-4f6c-b874-c2386fa6391a", "type": "concept", "title": "Common misinterpretations in abstracts and media reports"}, {"id": "45c6125e-a8c7-4cc2-b60b-2110f8ef5781", "type": "concept", "title": "CONSORT/STROBE-style reporting awareness"}]}]}, {"id": "40fabb41-fefc-40d1-b356-d97f3bea955e", "type": "topic", "title": "Statistical software, reproducible workflows, reporting, and research communication", "description": "Modern statistics is also a workflow. This section helps students use software responsibly, organize data, and communicate results in research-ready formats.", "children": [{"id": "4df16149-55b1-4b2a-93c5-ab3ef2cb2ccf", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Software workflows and reproducibility", "description": "Students learn practical habits that make analyses checkable and reusable.", "children": [{"id": "941baf51-70d9-4ca6-8839-2399c04b9c5a", "type": "concept", "title": "Spreadsheet hygiene, codebooks, data dictionaries, and versioning"}, {"id": "d5ab12ba-87cd-4efe-a36b-4865cf05ebec", "type": "concept", "title": "R, SPSS, JASP, jamovi, or spreadsheet analysis concepts"}, {"id": "ef0bd135-a211-452e-aba7-be4bf2cf27a9", "type": "concept", "title": "Data cleaning, recoding, formulas, scripts, and output verification"}, {"id": "4c5ec39e-4a10-40d6-b590-a1881ba6d416", "type": "concept", "title": "Reproducibility, file organization, and transparent decision logs"}]}, {"id": "94815173-6ad3-46cb-8de7-0b7f525e6f01", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Reporting and communication", "description": "The gist is to turn analysis into clear tables, figures, and written conclusions.", "children": [{"id": "e9e49827-123b-4128-8044-4d6da407def9", "type": "concept", "title": "APA and health-science reporting conventions"}, {"id": "ae51aeb5-a8ce-4122-bf7e-dc554f74cd3b", "type": "concept", "title": "Tables, figures, captions, and statistical notation"}, {"id": "88da0012-6439-401a-a583-93d5e1e4af24", "type": "concept", "title": "Plain-language conclusions with limitations, assumptions, and uncertainty"}]}]}], "dbBacked": true}$statjson$::jsonb,
  is_catalog = true,
  is_private = false,
  owner_id = null,
  kind = 'course',
  visibility = 'public',
  updated_at = now()
where slug = 'statistics'
  and (kind is null or kind = 'course');
